{
  "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
  "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How do -- what in the record shows us that the failure to tell him that by the lawyer was anything other than negligence? What in the record suggests that the lawyer, just as many lawyers do, forgot to call the client, forgot to send him something? What shows that this is more than negligence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0000-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It is undisputed that Petitioner was not provided notice that the State supreme court had denied his postconviction appeal and had issued its mandate, with the result being that his AEDPA statute of limitations expired."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Todd G. Scher (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Eleventh Circuit characterized Mr. Collins's conduct as, which was gross negligence. And what we have here is a confluence --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why should -- why should that be? Two cases. Two criminal defendants. One spends a lot of time trying to find the most competent lawyer he can, and he does. He finds a highly skilled lawyer, who makes one little mistake and it's negligence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What we have here is Petitioner putting the Florida Supreme Court on explicit notice that he is having a problem with his lawyer, and further -- earlier in that particular letter, he apologizes to the clerk, saying: I'm sorry to pester you with these -- with these requests, but if I had a lawyer who was responding to my letters and who was listening to me and who would send me the documentations, I wouldn't have to be bothering you, but this is the situation that I'm in."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is this case different if the filing error -- I understand there was a lot going on, but if the lawyer just miscalculated the days and was off by one day, this case comes out the other way in your view, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Then you are -- you seem, from what you just said, to be relying on a distinction between paid counsel, who is just as careless, and court-appointed counsel, because in the one case the client had picked that attorney, and in the other case, the client was given this attorney by the State. So I think you\u2019re suggesting that the State has some responsibility when it provides the counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You mean to imply that earthquake, fire, flood, mad postman burns mail truck, et cetera?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if the lawyer lies to the client and the client says my time is running out, have you filed my -- my Federal habeas petition, and the lawyer says, yes, I filed it and here it is, and it has a -- a forged date stamp on it? No equitable tolling there?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Authority to do this, would it make sense to say that the State is going to be subject to equitable tolling on a rather broad standard -- we\u2019re going to give equitable tolling often -- unless the State has attorney discipline procedures, so that this happens only once and then the attorney can no longer practice in the Federal courts?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Was that denied on the ground that he was pro se?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State -- the State has no responsibility even though it made this appointment? So you agree there's no difference, whether it was paid counsel, somebody that the -- that the defendant picked to represent him, and someone that he just had to take because it's what the State gave him?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Why could you not say here -- I mean, the key sentence, I take it, is the Eleventh Circuit and it says: \u201cNo allegation of lawyer negligence or failure to meet the standard of care\u201d -- none -- without \u201cproof, bad faith, dishonesty, mental impairment\u201d on the part of the lawyer, could ever qualify."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Makar",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MAKAR: Well, we agree with the Eleventh Circuit standard to the extent it says, you know, that this sort of attorney negligence, gross negligence, incompetence, is not enough. Where we differ from the Eleventh Circuit is we're concerned, based upon our pragmatic day-in, day-out handling of these cases, that when you say dishonesty, well -- or a conflict -- that those concepts can be conflated into things that they are not, particularly when these communications between lawyer and client are outside the State's view."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-09-5327",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d170aa8c7f1e7e94becff9af62ce3c2a77296b82bfcf2c3bbff3a935f6db6e4a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d170aa8c7f1e-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
